      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The mattress industry runs on permanent discounts. What matters is whether the price is lower than what the same mattress has sold for over the past few months, and whether the trial, return and warranty terms protect you if it is the wrong bed. This guide gives broad, approximate price ranges, the holiday sale calendar, and a checklist for telling a good deal from a staged one.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="mattress-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does a Mattress Cost? Typical Ranges by Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no single fair price for a mattress, but there are broad bands, and knowing them stops you being impressed by a discount off an inflated starting number. The figures below are rough queen-size ranges for what people commonly pay, not list prices. They are approximate, vary by size and retailer, and any given model can sit outside them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">All-Foam: The Widest Spread</h3>

          <p>
            Foam mattresses run from boxed beds costing a few hundred dollars for a queen to premium memory foam models well over a thousand. The low end has thinner comfort layers and lower-density foam that can soften sooner; the middle usually adds thicker transition layers and better edge feel. Our <a href="/best-mattress/">best mattress guide</a> covers foam picks at several price points.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid: The Mid-Range Default</h3>

          <p>
            Hybrids pair a pocketed coil base with foam or latex on top and tend to cost more than foam. A typical queen hybrid lands roughly in the high hundreds to around two thousand dollars, with luxury hybrids higher. Coils add bounce, airflow and edge support, which many heavier sleepers prefer; see <a href="/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/">mattresses for heavier people</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Innerspring, Latex and Adjustable Air</h3>

          <p>
            Basic innerspring beds can be among the cheapest options. Natural latex sits near the top of the market, with queens commonly in the low-to-mid thousands. Adjustable air beds with pumps and app control are the priciest category, often several thousand dollars with a base. A higher price buys features, not guaranteed comfort.
          </p>

