        <div id="prices-by-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Mattress Size Changes the Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most mattress pages quote a queen price first, but the price you pay depends heavily on size, and the steps between sizes vary by brand. Always click through to your exact size before comparing deals: a brand that looks cheap at queen can be expensive at king.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twin and Twin XL</h3>

          <p>
            Twin and twin XL are the cheapest sizes. Twin XL adds five inches of length and is the standard for dorms and split king setups. For a child, a dorm or a daybed, a budget foam model at this size is often enough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Full and Queen</h3>

          <p>
            Fulls typically cost a little less than queens, but the gap is often small, so many shoppers step up to queen. Queens get the most aggressive promotions, and headline discounts are usually calculated on a queen, so check that the deal applies to your size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">King, California King and Split King</h3>

          <p>
            King and California king usually carry a meaningful premium over queen. A split king, two twin XLs side by side, can cost more than a standard king but lets each partner choose a firmness. Larger sizes may also cost more to return.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheets, toppers and protectors scale with the bed too, so budget for the total. See our <a href="/best-mattress-protector/">mattress protector guide</a> and <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">topper guide</a> before buying add-ons at checkout.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sale-calendar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. When Do Mattresses Go on Sale? The US Sale Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mattress brands promote around long weekends and big retail events, and the pattern repeats every year. Specific offers change, so this guide does not list current deals. What stays constant is the rhythm: a handful of holiday windows bring the lowest prices, and prices often drift back up between them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Presidents' Day and Memorial Day</h3>

          <p>
            Presidents' Day in February is the first big mattress event of the year, and Memorial Day in late May is one of the most important. Promotions often start a week or more before the holiday and run a few days after.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">July 4 and Amazon Prime Day</h3>

          <p>
            Independence Day sales arrive in early July, and Amazon's Prime Day typically lands in July too. Prime Day deals apply to mattresses sold on Amazon and usually require a Prime membership, so midsummer is a good time to compare a brand's own site with its Amazon listing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Labor Day and Prime Big Deal Days</h3>

          <p>
            Labor Day in early September is another major mattress weekend. Amazon's fall event, Prime Big Deal Days, has run in October in recent years and works like a smaller Prime Day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Black Friday and Cyber Monday</h3>

          <p>
            Black Friday and Cyber Monday in late November are often the strongest mattress sales of the year, and many brands stretch them across most of the month. Early in November, buy only if the price already matches previous holiday lows.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
