        <div id="real-discount" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is the Mattress Discount Real? How to Judge a Sale
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest trap in mattress shopping is the anchor price. A mattress listed at a high "regular" price and permanently discounted is not on sale; the discounted number is its real price. The percentage on the banner tells you little. The price compared with that model's own history tells you a lot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look at Price History, Not the Banner</h3>

          <p>
            For mattresses sold on Amazon, a price tracking tool can show how a listing has moved over months. For brand sites, check the same page several times over a few weeks. If the "sale" price matches last month's price, it is the everyday price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for List-Price Inflation</h3>

          <p>
            Some retailers set a list price the mattress rarely sells at, so every promotion looks dramatic. Store chains also sell near-identical beds under exclusive names, which blocks direct comparison. When names differ, compare layer lists, coil type, height and warranty instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bundles and Free Gifts</h3>

          <p>
            A "free" pillow, sheet set or protector has value only if you would have bought it anyway and it is decent quality. Bundles often replace a cash discount, so ask whether money off the mattress alone would be worth more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Judge the price, not the percentage.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A mattress is a good deal when it costs less than it usually sells for and comes with a trial and return policy you can actually use. The size of the discount on the banner is marketing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="trial-periods" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Mattress Trial Periods and Returns: What "100 Nights" Really Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sleep trials are why buying a mattress online works. Most online brands offer a trial commonly somewhere from around 100 nights up to a year. The number of nights is the headline; the conditions are what matter, so read them before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Break-In Requirement</h3>

          <p>
            Many brands require you to keep the mattress for a minimum period, often around 30 nights, before starting a return, to give your body time to adjust. If you are buying on a deadline, know that you cannot return it in the first few weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Return Logistics and Fees</h3>

          <p>
            A boxed mattress cannot go back in its box, so returns usually involve a scheduled pickup or donation. Some brands charge a pickup fee or limit you to one return per household, and stores may charge restocking fees or allow only an exchange.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Amazon Returns vs Brand Trials</h3>

          <p>
            Mattresses bought on Amazon follow the return policy on that listing, which may differ from the brand's own sleep trial. Check the listing's return window and, if it matters, ask the brand whether its trial applies to marketplace orders.
          </p>

          <p>
            A long trial is not a substitute for choosing well. Start with sleep position and body weight: our guides to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/">best mattress for side sleepers</a> and the <a href="/best-mattress-for-back-pain/">best mattress for back pain</a> narrow the field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranties" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Mattress Warranties: What They Cover and What Voids Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            A trial covers comfort: you can send the bed back if you do not like it. A warranty covers defects as the maker defines them. Ten-year warranties are common and some brands advertise longer or "lifetime" coverage, but length alone does not tell you how useful it is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sagging and Body Impressions</h3>

          <p>
            The most important clause is usually the sag threshold. Warranties commonly cover body impressions only beyond a set depth, often around an inch to an inch and a half, measured with no one on the bed. Softening that stays below the threshold is treated as normal wear. Compare thresholds across brands; a smaller number means you are more likely to qualify for a claim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prorated vs Non-Prorated Coverage</h3>

          <p>
            A non-prorated period means the maker repairs or replaces a defective mattress at no charge beyond shipping. A prorated period means you pay a growing share of the cost as the years pass. Many "lifetime" warranties are mostly prorated, so the long number is less generous than it sounds. Read which years are fully covered.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foundation, Stains and Other Exclusions</h3>

          <p>
            Warranties typically require proper support, such as a solid platform or slats spaced within a set gap, and may require a center support on larger sizes. Stains and soiling commonly void coverage, which is why a waterproof protector is worth using from the first night. Keep proof of purchase and the law label; removing that label can complicate a claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Established makers tend to have clearer warranty terms. Our <a href="/best-mattress-brands/">best mattress brands guide</a> compares brands on trial, warranty and return terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="online-vs-store" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying a Mattress Online vs in a Store
          </h2>

          <p>
            Online brands usually win on price and trial length; stores win on letting you lie on the bed before you pay. Many shoppers combine the two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying Online</h3>

          <p>
            Boxed mattresses ship compressed to your door, often free, with a long trial. Prices are easier to compare because the same model name appears on the brand site and on Amazon. You cannot test first, so the return terms carry more weight. Expect some off-gassing smell for the first few days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying in a Store</h3>

          <p>
            A showroom lets you feel firmness, edge support and motion transfer before you commit. Lie on each candidate in your usual sleep position for several minutes. Store prices are often negotiable around holidays, and delivery and old-mattress removal may be included. The trade-off is store-exclusive model names and sometimes stricter returns.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Hybrid Approach</h3>

          <p>
            Use a store to learn which feel you like, then shop that category online when a sale window comes around. Some online brands have showrooms or partner stores, so you can try the exact model first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Skip When Buying a Mattress
          </h2>

          <p>
            Checkout pages are designed to add items to your cart. Some add-ons are worth having; many are not, or cost far less elsewhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip: Paying for Labels Over Specs</h3>

          <p>
            Words like "orthopedic", "luxury" and "hotel quality" have no fixed meaning. Compare foam densities, coil type, layer thickness and certifications instead. Many people with back pain prefer medium-firm support, but comfort is individual, so talk to your doctor or physical therapist about persistent pain rather than relying on a marketing label. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-seniors/">mattress guide for seniors</a> covers edge support and ease of getting up as well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip: Matching Foundations You Do Not Need</h3>

          <p>
            If your existing platform bed, slatted frame or foundation is solid and meets the warranty spacing rules, you probably do not need the matching base. Box springs designed for old innerspring beds may not suit a foam or hybrid mattress, but a new one is only worth buying if your current support fails the warranty requirements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip: Extended Protection Plans</h3>

          <p>
            Retailer protection plans often overlap with the manufacturer warranty. Frequently the extra coverage is stains and accidental damage, which a waterproof protector largely prevents for far less money.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip: Buying Under Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            "Ends tonight" banners are common, and the same offer often returns at the next holiday. If a deal is not clearly better than the model's usual price, wait. The sale calendar above means another chance is rarely more than a couple of months away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Percentage Off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mattress brands run discounts most of the year, so a large percentage off a high list price is often just the everyday price. Judge a deal by what the same model has sold for over recent months, not by the size of the number on the banner. Price history beats marketing every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Without Reading the Return Terms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A long trial means little if the return includes pickup fees, a mandatory break-in period, or a one-return limit. Read the conditions before you pay, and confirm whether a brand trial applies when you buy through Amazon or another retailer rather than the brand website directly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Queen Prices When You Need a King</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Headline discounts are usually quoted on a queen. The gap between sizes varies by brand, so a mattress that looks cheaper at queen may cost more at king or split king. Always price your exact size, plus the sheets and protector to match, before comparing offers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a Mattress Protector</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stains commonly void mattress warranties, and a spill can also complicate a trial return. A waterproof protector costs little compared with the bed it protects. Put one on from the first night so the warranty and the return option stay available if you need them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress deal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best mattress deal is a price below what that specific model usually sells for, on the size you need, with a trial and return policy you can actually use. Holiday windows such as Memorial Day, Labor Day and Black Friday tend to bring the lowest prices. For product picks rather than deals, start with our best mattress guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to buy a mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Major US holiday weekends are the best times: Presidents' Day, Memorial Day, July 4, Labor Day and Black Friday through Cyber Monday. Amazon's Prime Day in July and Prime Big Deal Days in the fall also bring discounts on mattresses sold there. Prices between those windows often drift back up toward their usual level.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on a mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very roughly, a decent queen foam mattress can cost a few hundred dollars, a mid-range foam or hybrid commonly sits in the high hundreds to around two thousand, and latex or adjustable air beds cost more. Prices vary by size and retailer. Spend enough to get the support your body weight and sleep position need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are mattress sales real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are and some are not. Many brands keep a permanent promotion running, so the sale price is effectively the regular price. A sale is real when the price drops below what the same model has sold for over recent months. Check price history or the brand page over several weeks before trusting the banner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you return a mattress bought on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes, but the terms depend on the listing. Amazon's return window and the brand's own sleep trial are not always the same thing. Check the return policy on the listing before buying, and confirm with the brand whether its full trial applies to Amazon orders. Keep the order details and the law label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mattress warranties cover sagging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but only beyond a set depth, commonly around an inch to an inch and a half measured with no weight on the bed. Shallower softening is treated as normal wear. Warranties also typically require proper foundation support and a clean, unstained mattress, so a protector and a suitable frame help keep coverage valid.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

